##Some Basic Git Commands:-
1) git init: It creates a new, empty git repository or reinitializes an existing one

2) git clone: It copies a repository from GitHub

3) git status: It displays the current state of your Git working directory and staging area.

4) git add: It moves changes from your working directory to the staging area

5) git commit: It captures a local snapshot of your project's currently staged changes and permanently records it to your repository's history

6) git log: It displays the commit history of a Git repository

7) git diff: It displays the exact line-by-line differences between different states of your repository

8) git push: It uploads your local repository commits to a remote repository

9) git pull: It downloads the latest changes from a remote repository and immediately integrates them into your local working branch

10) git fetch: It downloads commits, files, and tracking references from a remote repository into your local repository, without modifying your current working files or active branch

11) git branch: It is a branch management tool that allows you to list, create, rename, and delete branches within a Git repository

12) git switch: It is used to navigate between branches in a Git repository
